name: archives
boss: Clobber
flavor: Unpack me carelessly and I scatter across everything you own.
brief: tar bundles many files into one. It does not compress by itself -- -z
brief: pipes the bundle through gzip, -j through bzip2. That is why you see
brief: czf and xzf so often: create/extract, compress, file.
brief:
brief: Always look before you extract. tar tf lists the contents without
brief: writing anything, and an archive that expands into the current
brief: directory instead of a subdirectory will scatter files everywhere.
brief:
brief: -c here means stdout, not count.
---
setup: you just downloaded @archive@ and don't trust it yet
q: %list% @archive@'s contents without extracting anything
a: tar -tf @archive@
out: ^[A-Za-z0-9_./-]+/?$
ref: tar
teach: tar t
teach: tar -f
hint: t lists, and -f names the archive file to read
---
q: %list% @archive@'s contents with sizes and permissions
a: tar -tvf @archive@
out: ^[-d][-rwx]{9}
ref: tar
teach: tar -v
hint: v adds the verbose per-entry detail, sizes and permissions
---
q: %make% bundle.tar containing @dir@
a: tar -cf bundle.tar @dir@
state: exists:bundle.tar
ref: tar
teach: tar c
hint: c creates a new archive; f names the file to write it to
---
q: %make% a gzip-compressed bundle.tar.gz containing @dir@
a: tar -czf bundle.tar.gz @dir@
state: exists:bundle.tar.gz
ref: tar
teach: tar -z
hint: z pipes the archive through gzip as it's written
---
q: %make% a bzip2-compressed bundle.tar.bz2 containing @dir@
a: tar -cjf bundle.tar.bz2 @dir@
state: exists:bundle.tar.bz2
ref: tar
teach: tar -j
hint: j pipes the archive through bzip2 instead of gzip
---
q: %get% archive/export.tar into the current directory
a: tar -xf archive/export.tar
why: fixture-cost
ref: tar
teach: tar x
hint: x extracts; with no -C it lands in the current directory
---
q: %get% @archive@ into @dir@, not scattered here
a: tar -xzf @archive@ -C @dir@
why: fixture-cost
ref: tar
teach: tar -C
hint: -C changes into that directory before extracting
---
q: {peek at src/main.c in @archive@ without extracting it all|read src/main.c out of @archive@ to your terminal|check src/main.c before extracting the rest of @archive@}
a: tar -xzOf @archive@ src/main.c
why: fixture-cost
ref: tar
teach: tar -O
hint: O sends the extracted file's bytes to stdout instead of writing it to disk
---
q: %make% bundle.tar from @dir@ but leave out anything ending in .log
a: tar -cf bundle.tar --exclude '*.log' @dir@
state: exists:bundle.tar
ref: tar
teach: tar --exclude
hint: --exclude takes a pattern and skips anything that matches it
---
q: %get% @archive@ without its top-level wrapper
a: tar -xzf @archive@ --strip-components 1
why: fixture-cost
ref: tar
teach: tar --strip-components
hint: --strip-components N removes that many leading path segments
---
setup: your last extraction of @archive@ half-finished and left files behind
q: %get% @archive@ overwriting anything already in the way
a: tar -xzf @archive@ --overwrite
why: fixture-cost
ref: tar
teach: tar --overwrite
hint: --overwrite lets extraction replace files already in the way
---
q: %make% bundle.tar from the list of names held in names.txt
a: tar -cf bundle.tar -T names.txt
state: exists:bundle.tar
ref: tar
teach: tar -T
hint: -T reads the list of names to archive from a file instead of the command line
---
q: {bundle @dir@ without descending into its subdirectories|use tar --no-recursion so @dir@'s contents aren't walked into|archive @dir@ as a flat entry, skipping what's inside it}
a: tar -cf bundle.tar --no-recursion @dir@
state: exists:bundle.tar
ref: tar
teach: tar --no-recursion
hint: --no-recursion archives a directory entry itself without descending into it
---
q: %get% @archive@ without trusting its recorded modes
a: tar -xzf @archive@ --no-same-permissions
why: fixture-cost
ref: tar
teach: tar --no-same-permissions
hint: --no-same-permissions applies your umask instead of the recorded modes
---
q: %get% @archive@ without resolving owners to fake names
a: tar -xzf @archive@ --numeric-owner
why: fixture-cost
ref: tar
teach: tar --numeric-owner
hint: --numeric-owner keeps uid/gid numbers instead of resolving to names
---
q: {compress @txt@ with gzip, replacing it with @txt@.gz|use gzip on @txt@, which becomes @txt@.gz in place|gzip @txt@ so the original is replaced by its .gz}
a: gzip @txt@
state: exists:@txt@.gz
ref: gzip
teach: gzip
hint: gzip replaces the original file with a .gz version by default
---
q: {compress @txt@ but you still need the original file afterward|gzip @txt@ without losing @txt@ itself|compress @txt@ down to .gz, but keep @txt@ around too}
a: gzip -k @txt@
state: exists:@txt@.gz
ref: gzip
teach: gzip -k
hint: -k keeps the original file alongside the new .gz
---
q: {compress @txt@ into out.gz without modifying or removing @txt@ itself|you want the compressed bytes in out.gz, @txt@ left completely alone|send @txt@'s compressed form to out.gz, leaving the original untouched}
a: gzip -c @txt@ > out.gz
state: exists:out.gz
ref: gzip
teach: gzip -c
hint: -c writes the compressed bytes to stdout, leaving the file untouched
---
q: {a leftover @txt@.gz exists -- compress @txt@ anyway|@txt@.gz already exists -- redo the compression|compress @txt@ again though its .gz is already there}
a: gzip -f @txt@
state: exists:@txt@.gz
ref: gzip
teach: gzip -f
hint: -f overrides gzip's refusal to clobber an existing .gz
---
q: {gzip @txt@ to z.gz, then read it without unpacking to disk|print z.gz's contents, gzipped from @txt@, without writing anything|stream z.gz's contents, built from @txt@, to your terminal}
a: gzip -c @txt@ > z.gz && zcat z.gz
out: rotate
ref: zcat
teach: zcat
hint: zcat is gzip -dc -- decompress straight to stdout
---
q: {compress @txt@ into z.gz, then uncompress it back to z|use gunzip to turn z.gz, gzipped from @txt@, back into z|reverse gzip's compression on z.gz, built from @txt@}
a: gzip -c @txt@ > z.gz && gunzip z.gz
state: exists:z
ref: gunzip
teach: gunzip
hint: gunzip is the inverse of gzip, replacing the .gz with the original
---
q: {make z.gz from @txt@, then confirm it isn't corrupt|verify that z.gz, gzipped from @txt@, decompresses cleanly, untouched|check that z.gz, from @txt@, isn't corrupt, no output written}
a: gzip -c @txt@ > z.gz && gunzip -t z.gz
status: 0
ref: gunzip
teach: gunzip -t
hint: -t verifies the archive's integrity without writing any output
---
q: {gzip @txt@ to z.gz, then print its contents, leaving z.gz in place|read z.gz's contents, gzipped from @txt@, without deleting it|stream z.gz's contents to stdout, z.gz left untouched}
a: gzip -c @txt@ > z.gz && gunzip -c z.gz
out: rotate
ref: gunzip
teach: gunzip -c
hint: -c decompresses to stdout, leaving the .gz file in place
---
q: {compress @txt@ with bzip2 rather than gzip|use bzip2 instead of gzip on @txt@|run bzip2 on @txt@ in place of gzip}
a: bzip2 @txt@
state: exists:@txt@.bz2
ref: bzip2
teach: bzip2
hint: bzip2 works like gzip but usually compresses tighter, at more cpu cost
---
q: {@txt@ needs to be as small as possible -- max bzip2 strength|squeeze @txt@ down as far as bzip2 can, cpu cost be damned|compress @txt@ with bzip2's strongest, slowest setting}
a: bzip2 -9 @txt@
state: exists:@txt@.bz2
ref: bzip2
teach: bzip2 -1..9
hint: -9 is the tightest compression level; -1 is the fastest
---
q: {compress @txt@ into out.bz2 without modifying @txt@ itself|send @txt@'s bzip2-compressed bytes to out.bz2, original left alone|bzip2 @txt@ down to out.bz2, keeping the source file as is}
a: bzip2 -c @txt@ > out.bz2
state: exists:out.bz2
ref: bzip2
teach: bzip2 -c
hint: -c writes the compressed bytes to stdout instead of replacing the file
---
q: {bzip2 @txt@ to z.bz2, then uncompress it back|use bunzip2 to reverse the .bz2 compression of @txt@|decompress z.bz2, bzip2'd from @txt@, back to its original}
a: bzip2 -c @txt@ > z.bz2 && bunzip2 z.bz2
state: exists:z
ref: bunzip2
teach: bunzip2
hint: bunzip2 is bzip2's inverse, same relationship gunzip has to gzip
---
q: {bzip2 @txt@ to z.bz2, then confirm it isn't corrupt|verify that z.bz2, bzip2'd from @txt@, decompresses cleanly, untouched|make sure z.bz2, from @txt@, isn't corrupt, no output written}
a: bzip2 -c @txt@ > z.bz2 && bunzip2 -t z.bz2
status: 0
ref: bunzip2
teach: bunzip2 -t
hint: -t tests integrity without writing any decompressed output
---
setup: you just downloaded archive.zip
q: {unpack it into the current directory|extract archive.zip here, no target directory needed|extract every file in archive.zip right here}
a: unzip archive.zip
state: exists:hello.txt
ref: unzip
teach: unzip
hint: unzip with no flags extracts everything into the current directory
---
q: %list% what is inside a zip without extracting it
a: unzip -l archive.zip
out: hello.txt
ref: unzip
teach: unzip -l
hint: -l lists contents without extracting anything
---
q: {unpack archive.zip into @dir@ instead of scattering it here|keep archive.zip's contents contained -- extract it into @dir@|extract archive.zip somewhere specific, @dir@, not here}
a: unzip archive.zip -d @dir@
state: exists:@dir@/hello.txt
ref: unzip
teach: unzip -d
hint: -d names the directory to extract into instead of the current one
---
q: {old files are in the way -- re-extract archive.zip, overwrite them|redo the extraction of archive.zip, replacing what's already there|extract archive.zip again without it asking about existing files}
a: unzip -o archive.zip
state: exists:hello.txt
ref: unzip
teach: unzip -o
hint: -o overwrites existing files without prompting for confirmation
---
q: %show% one file out of a zip to stdout instead of writing it
a: unzip -p archive.zip hello.txt
out: hello from the archive
ref: unzip
teach: unzip -p
hint: -p extracts one named file to stdout instead of writing it
---
q: {you just downloaded archive.zip -- make sure it isn't corrupt|verify archive.zip is intact without extracting anything|check archive.zip's integrity before you unpack it}
a: unzip -t archive.zip
status: 0
ref: unzip
teach: unzip -t
hint: -t tests the archive's integrity without extracting anything
